# Sanitized Phase 2 physical IMU quaternion interpretation evidence

Evidence ID: IMU-ORIENT-001-R01
Date reported: 2026-08-17
Branch: fix/02-unitree-driver-and-tf
Tested direct-mapping commit: 543a218150245eaac8ebfbefc0cc928ebebdd905
Result: FAIL for direct SDK[0,1,2,3] -> ROS (x,y,z,w) interpretation
Correction status: SDK[1,2,3,0] -> ROS (x,y,z,w) implemented in an uncommitted working tree; corrected physical retest later passed as IMU-ORIENT-001-R02

## Reported environment and physical arrangement

- Raspberry Pi 4
- Ubuntu 22.04.5 Jammy
- Linux aarch64
- ROS 2 Humble
- physical Unitree LiDAR connected over UDP
- Pi eth0: 192.168.1.2
- LiDAR: 192.168.1.62
- sensor upright
- cable outlet toward robot rear
- LiDAR +X approximately robot-forward
- LiDAR +Z approximately robot-up
- physical model at this initial test: not reported; later identified as Unitree L2
- exact hardware revision and firmware: unconfirmed
- complete launch command, parameter-file hash, and raw terminal transcript: not supplied
- motor/safety state: not reported; no motor or motion-behavior claim is made

The checked-in software is based on Unitree SDK2 version 2.0.9 for the L2-family software lineage. That lineage did not identify the sensor by itself; later physical closeout evidence separately identified the tested sensor as a Unitree L2.

## Physical procedure

1. Hold the robot/sensor stationary in Position A and record the four published values produced by the then-current direct mapping.
2. Rotate the stationary robot/sensor approximately 90 degrees clockwise on the floor when viewed from above.
3. Hold it stationary in Position B and record the four published values.
4. Compare the relative rotation under the direct and reordered interpretations.

