## Failed direct interpretation

Mapping:

ROS x = SDK[0]
ROS y = SDK[1]
ROS z = SDK[2]
ROS w = SDK[3]

Relative rotation, Position B times inverse(Position A):

angle = approximately 99.18 degrees
axis = approximately [0.9992, 0.0254, -0.0296]

This failed the physical test because the observed planar clockwise motion appeared almost entirely as a +X rotation.

## Hardware-supported project interpretation

Mapping:

ROS x = SDK[1]
ROS y = SDK[2]
ROS z = SDK[3]
ROS w = SDK[0]

Position A roll/pitch/yaw:

roll = approximately +0.80 degrees
pitch = approximately -2.42 degrees
yaw = approximately +8.21 degrees

Position B roll/pitch/yaw:

roll = approximately +2.28 degrees
pitch = approximately -2.23 degrees
yaw = approximately -91.02 degrees

Relative rotation, Position B times inverse(Position A):

angle = approximately 99.18 degrees
axis = approximately [0.0141, -0.0095, -0.9999]

This matches the physical clockwise planar rotation around robot/LiDAR -Z. It supports the array-to-component mapping. It does not by itself verify the quaternion's absolute reference frame, arbitrary 3D sign/direction semantics, the dynamic TF parent-child interpretation, or the static IMU-to-LiDAR extrinsic.

## Vendor-source inconsistency

- Checked-in SDK2 `include/example.h` labels `imu.quaternion[0..3]` as `(x,y,z,w)`.
- The historical/upstream Unitree ROS IMU publisher assigns `[0,1,2,3]` directly to ROS `(x,y,z,w)`.
- The historical/upstream Unitree dynamic TF assigns `[1,2,3,0]` to ROS `(x,y,z,w)`.
- The physical test supports the dynamic-TF reordering, not the direct mapping.

The vendor sources are therefore internally inconsistent; this artifact does not claim otherwise.

## Coexisting point-cloud observation

During this test, valid clouds were reported as publishing steadily at approximately 11.96 Hz while the driver repeatedly logged:

Unitree SDK reported a point packet but did not return a point cloud

Because valid output remained fresh, that recurring condition is not classified as a hardware failure or loss of cloud transport. The focused correction changes only diagnostic severity: an individual getter miss is debug-throttled while a valid cloud has been published recently and remains a warning when valid-cloud freshness is lost. SDK parsing and cloud conversion are unchanged. Later Phase 2 testing passed repeated cloud/IMU publication, clean restart, physical Ethernet reconnect, and post-reboot operation; external timestamp accuracy and long-duration performance limits remain unverified.

## Subsequent corrected retest

The corrected wrapper was rebuilt on ARM64/Humble and physically retested as `IMU-ORIENT-001-R02`. The new Position A/B samples produced an approximately 89.755-degree relative rotation around `[0.01589, -0.00884, -0.99983]`, and the IMU message and dynamic TF used the same corrected normalized quaternion. This passed the planar mapping test and did not regress to the direct-mapping `+X` result.

The sanitized final samples, build result, publication measurements, networking root cause, restart, disconnect/reconnect, and reboot results are preserved in `docs/verification/artifacts/phase2-final-physical-resilience_2026-08-20.log`. The planar pass does not claim globally referenced heading, arbitrary 3D accuracy, or measured robot extrinsics.
